{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n<description>\nDrop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite pixels.\n- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n  pixel it crossed.\n- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\nbottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1b8318e3\" lines=\"86-105\">\n<description>\nMove each isolated colored pixel into the one-cell frame around a grey 2x2 block.\n\nInput structure:\n- Background is the dominant color.\n- Grey foreground objects are solid 2x2 anchor blocks.\n- Every other non-background, non-grey cell is a colored singleton source.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the grey anchors fixed and clear all colored singleton source pixels.\n- For each singleton and each anchor, the normal destination is the cell reached by\n  clamping the pixel into the anchor's one-cell outer frame.\n- If a diagonally outside singleton has an exact 8-direction sightline into an anchor,\n  also allow the cell just before the first grey pixel on that ray.\n- Choose distinct destinations that minimize total king-move distance; at equal\n  distance prefer exact sightlines, then the shorter Manhattan movement.\n\nKey insight: The grey 2x2 blocks define magnetic frames; conflicts are resolved\nstructurally by nearest-frame assignment rather than by color identity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\" lines=\"42-53\">\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"41ace6b5\" lines=\"7-26\">\n<description>\nNormalize vertical two-color bars between paired marker columns around the marker rows.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- A row of red marker pixels sits directly above a row of gray marker pixels\n  in the same alternating columns.\n- The columns between adjacent markers contain vertical bars made from a\n  contiguous teal run above a contiguous blue run.\n\nRule\n- Keep the marker pixels and all non-bar background cells fixed.\n- Let h be the maximum teal-run height among the bar columns; paint teal in\n  every bar column for h rows ending at the red marker row.\n- In each bar column, move its blue run to start at the gray marker row and\n  fill all lower cells in that column with maroon.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker rows are a baseline: teal is normalized into a common upper\n  band, while each blue run keeps its own height below the baseline.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"edcc2ff0\" lines=\"47-63\">\n<description>\nKeep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers\nin the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has\na unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\nIn the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground\nobject to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0\nwhose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.\n\nKey insight\nThe top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects\nsurvive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\" lines=\"102-115\">\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17b866bd\" lines=\"49-67\">\n<description>\nMove each marker from a lattice corner gap into the adjacent enclosed 12-pixel cavity.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a repeated teal lattice around black 12-pixel cavities.\n- A marker is a non-black pixel placed at the northwest corner gap of one\n  complete 5x5 lattice tile; the marker may itself be teal.\n\nRule:\n- For every marked northwest corner gap, clear that marker to black.\n- Fill the 12 black pixels in the tile immediately down-right of the marker\n  with the marker color.\n- Leave the rest of the lattice unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The apparent isolated markers are control pixels for the adjacent cavity;\n  teal markers are recognized as extra teal pixels occupying a gap that the\n  periodic lattice template says should be black.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f215267\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nCount same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\n- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all small patterns from the grid.\n- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\n- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\n\nKey insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \"absorbed\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5ecac7f7\" lines=\"23-36\">\n<description>\nCombine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\n- The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\n- Build a new HxH background canvas.\n- From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\n\nKey insight\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
